% === provenance: PythonRandom ===
% CLAIM: Python's random module is deterministic: the same seed makes it
%   produce the same sequence again, and without a seed it takes its
%   starting point from the system time or the operating system's
%   randomness source -- which is why a program that draws random numbers
%   behaves differently on every run unless the seed is set.
% FOUND-VIA (here): known-item, primary source.  A claim about what
%   Python's random module does is answered by the module's own manual.
% PICKED: the library reference over the many tutorials that repeat it,
%   and over the CPython source, since the documented contract is what
%   the material relies on.
% QUOTE: "Python uses the Mersenne Twister as the core generator."  And:
%   "The generator's random() method will continue to produce the same
%   sequence when the compatible seeder is given the same seed."  And, on
%   seed(a): "If a is omitted or None, the current system time is used.
%   If randomness sources are provided by the operating system, they are
%   used instead of the system time".  And: "However, being completely
%   deterministic, it is not suitable for all purposes, and is completely
%   unsuitable for cryptographic purposes."  On randint: "Return a random
%   integer N such that a <= N <= b."
% VERIFIED (here): full text of
%   https://docs.python.org/3/library/random.html read 2026-09-03; every
%   quoted sentence appears there verbatim.
% COUNTER: not applicable -- the source documents the module it is cited
%   for.  The material reproduces the source's own qualification (the
%   generator is deterministic and unsuitable for cryptography) rather
%   than presenting seeding as a general-purpose guarantee.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
@online{PythonRandom,
  author       = {{Python Software Foundation}},
  title        = {random --- Generate pseudo-random numbers},
  subtitle     = {The Python Standard Library},
  url          = {https://docs.python.org/3/library/random.html},
  urldate      = {2026-09-03},
  year         = {2026},
}



